### Suggested Fix:
To handle null objects gracefully, you can modify the constructors and methods to check for null and return the string `"<null>"` instead of throwing an exception. For example:

```java
// In ReflectionToStringBuilder class
public ReflectionToStringBuilder(Object object, ToStringStyle style, StringBuffer buffer) {
    super(object == null ? "<null>" : Objects.requireNonNull(object, "obj"), style, buffer);
}

// In ReflectionToStringBuilder class
public static String toString(Object object, ToStringStyle style, boolean outputTransients, boolean outputStatics, Class<?> reflectUpToClass) {
    if (object == null) {
        return "<null>";
    }
    return new ReflectionToStringBuilder(object, style, null, reflectUpToClass, outputTransients, outputStatics).toString();
}
```

This way, the tests `testReflectionNull` and `testConstructorWithNullObject` should pass, returning `"<null>"` when the object is null.
